+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"0bd40bfa-19be-43d4-b649-655886501952","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to write the tests first, then the implementation. Let me create the test file, then implementation.\n\nLet me set up the test file and then write the implementation.\n\nFor the test file, I'll put it somewhere vitest can find it. Looking at vitest.config.ts, it uses default settings so tests should be matched by the default vitest glob pattern (which includes `**/*.test.ts` or `**/*.spec.ts`).\n\nLet me create the test file and implementation.\n\nLet me think about the algorithm more carefully:\n\nThe key challenge is \"maximizing the number of events scheduled.\" This is a scheduling optimization problem. The approach I'll use:\n\n1. **Cycle detection**: DFS with white/gray/black coloring\n2. **Topological sort**: Level-based (longest path length from sources)\n3. **Placement strategy**: Process events level by level, within each level sort by \"most constrained\" (fewest compatible rooms)\n4. **Room assignment**: For each event, find earliest valid slot across all compatible rooms\n\nFor placement, I need to:\n- Track room availability: for each room, track occupied intervals\n- Track event assignments: for each event, track start/end time\n- When placing an event, check all constraints:\n  a. Room capacity (attendees ≤ capacity)\n  b. Time window (start + duration ≤ endTime, start ≥ startTime)\n  c. Room gaps (no overlap with existing events in the room, respecting gap)\n  d. Prerequisites (all prereqs finish before start - gap)\n\nFor the \"earliest slot\" in a room, I can merge available intervals:\n- Start with [startTime, endTime] as the available window\n- For each existing assignment in the room, subtract occupied + gap from available windows\n- Find the first window where the event fits\n\nLet me write the implementation now.\n\nActually, let me think more about the algorithm for finding available slots in a room:\n\nGiven a room with existing assignments:\n```\nassignments = [{start: 30, end: 60}, {start: 90, end: 120}]\ngap = 15\n```\n\nAvailable slots (considering gap):\n- [startTime, 30-15] = [startTime, 15]\n- [60+15, 90-15] = [75, 75]\n- [120+15, endTime]\n\nFor an event of duration 30:\n- Slot 1: can start at startTime if startTime + 30 ≤ 15 (i.e., startTime ≤ -15, usually no)\n- Slot 2: can start at 75 if 75 + 30 ≤ 90 (i.e., 105 ≤ 90, no)\n- Slot 3: can start at 135 if 135 + 30 ≤ endTime\n\nAlso need to check prerequisite constraints:\n- e.g., if event has prereq that ends at 50, then start must be ≥ 50 + gap = 65\n\nSo the earliest start for the event considering prerequisites and the first available slot is:\n- earliest_possible = max(startTime, max(prereq_ends) + gap)\n- Then find the first available window where earliest_possible + duration ≤ window_end\n\nLet me implement this:\n\n```typescript\nfunction findEarliestSlot(\n  roomId: string,\n  duration: number,\n  prereqEndTimes: number[],\n  assignments: Map<string, Assignment[]>,\n  options: ScheduleOptions\n): { start: number; end: number } | null {\n  const roomAssignments = assignments.get(roomId) || [];\n  const sorted = [...roomAssignments].sort((a, b) => a.startTime - b.startTime);\n  \n  const gap = options.gap ?? 15;\n  const earliestPossible = Math.max(\n    options.startTime,\n    ...prereqEndTimes.map(t => t + gap)\n  );\n  \n  // If no existing assignments, try starting at earliestPossible\n  if (sorted.length === 0) {\n    if (earliestPossible + duration <= options.endTime) {\n      return { start: earliestPossible, end: earliestPossible + duration };\n    }\n    return null;\n  }\n  \n  // Find first available gap\n  // Check before first event\n  const firstEnd = sorted[0].startTime - gap;\n  if (earliestPossible + duration <= firstEnd) {\n    const start = Math.max(earliestPossible, options.startTime);\n    if (start + duration <= firstEnd) {\n      return { start, end: start + duration };\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Check between events\n  for (let i = 0; i < sorted.length - 1; i++) {\n    const gapStart = sorted[i].endTime + gap;\n    const gapEnd = sorted[i + 1].startTime - gap;\n    \n    if (gapEnd < gapStart) continue;\n    \n    const start = Math.max(earliestPossible, gapStart);\n    if (start + duration <= gapEnd) {\n      return { start, end: start + duration };\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Check after last event\n  const afterEnd = sorted[sorted.length - 1].endTime + gap;\n  const start = Math.max(earliestPossible, afterEnd);\n  if (start + duration <= options.endTime) {\n    return { start, end: start + duration };\n  }\n  \n  return null;\n}\n```\n\nNow, for the topological ordering and level computation:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeLevels(events: Event[]): Map<string, number> {\n  // Kahn's algorithm or DFS-based level computation\n  // Level = maximum distance from a source node (no prerequisites)\n  \n  const adj = new Map<string, string[]>(); // event id -> prerequisites (incoming edges)\n  const revAdj = new Map<string, string[]>(); // event id -> dependents (outgoing edges)\n  \n  for (const event of events) {\n    adj.set(event.id, event.prerequisites?.filter(p => events.some(e => e.id === p)) || []);\n    // Build reverse adjacency\n    for (const prereqId of adj.get(event.id)!) {\n      if (!revAdj.has(prereqId)) revAdj.set(prereqId, []);\n      revAdj.get(prereqId)!.push(event.id);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // BFS level computation (longest path)\n  const levels = new Map<string, number>();\n  const queue: string[] = [];\n  \n  for (const event of events) {\n    if (adj.get(event.id)!.length === 0) {\n      levels.set(event.id, 0);\n      queue.push(event.id);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // ... BFS to compute levels\n}\n```\n\nActually, let me think about the level computation more carefully.\n\nFor topological ordering respecting prerequisites, level 0 events have no prerequisites. Level 1 events have all prerequisites at level 0. Level N events have at least one prerequisite at level N-1 (and all others at level < N).\n\nTo compute levels:\n1. Start with events that have no unresolved prerequisites (level 0)\n2. Remove them from the dependency graph\n3. Repeat: events whose prerequisites have all been removed are level 1, etc.\n\nThis is essentially Kahn's algorithm for topological sort:\n\n```typescript\nfunction computeLevels(events: Event[]): Map<string, number> {\n  const levels = new Map<string, number>();\n  const inDegree = new Map<string, number>(); // number of unresolved prerequisites\n  const adj = new Map<string, string[]>(); // prereq -> [dependent events]\n  \n  for (const e of events) {\n    const prereqs = e.prerequisites?.filter(p => events.some(ev => ev.id === p)) || [];\n    inDegree.set(e.id, prereqs.length);\n    if (!adj.has(e.id)) adj.set(e.id, []);\n    for (const p of prereqs) {\n      if (!adj.has(p)) adj.set(p, []);\n      adj.get(p)!.push(e.id);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const queue: string[] = [];\n  for (const e of events) {\n    if (inDegree.get(e.id) === 0) {\n      queue.push(e.id);\n      levels.set(e.id, 0);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    const current = queue.shift()!;\n    for (const dep of (adj.get(current) || [])) {\n      inDegree.set(dep, inDegree.get(dep)! - 1);\n      if (inDegree.get(dep) === 0) {\n        levels.set(dep, levels.get(current)! + 1);\n        queue.push(dep);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return levels;\n}\n```\n\nNow, for the sorting and optimization. Events within the same level (no dependencies on each other) can be placed in any order. To maximize the number scheduled, I should place more constrained events first:\n\nConstrainedness factors:\n- Fewer compatible rooms (more constrained)\n- Longer duration (less flexible)\n- More prerequisites (already handled by levels)\n\nLet me think about how to compute constrainedness within a level:\n\nFor each event, count how many rooms can accommodate it (capacity >= attendees). Events with fewer options should be placed first.\n\n```typescript\nfunction constrainedness(event: Event, rooms: Room[]): number {\n  return rooms.filter(r => r.capacity >= (event.attendees || 1)).length;\n}\n```\n\nSort by constrainedness ascending (fewest options first), then by duration descending (longer events are harder to place), then by ID for stability.\n\nNow let me write the actual code.\n\nOne more thing: for the \"prerequisite references a missing event\" criterion — I need to decide how to handle this. The specification says prerequisites that reference non-existent events should be silently ignored (they can't form cycles since they don't reference any real event). I'll filter out non-existent prerequisite references.\n\nLet me now write the full implementation.","signature":"0bd40bfa-19be-43d4-b649-655886501952"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":135,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":27008,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c86ff3d8-4449-4d6c-807f-e14f64a50e0d","uuid":"0d66d855-c83c-4c0c-8c47-59fdaa68ec8e"}
