window correctly.\n\nLet me now write the complete implementation file.\n\nActually, one more thought. The `findEarliestSlotInRoom` function needs to know about the event's prerequisites to compute earliestAllowed. Let me pass the event.\n\nLet me also make sure I handle the case where `gap` is 0 or undefined correctly. Default should be 15.\n\nAlso, let me think about the \"batch optimization\" more carefully. The requirement says \"the scheduler should consider how placing one event affects the ability to place others.\" My approach of sorting by constrainedness within level is one way to do this. But there might be cases where greedy fails.\n\nFor example:\n- Room 1 (cap 10), Room 2 (cap 10)\n- Event A: 30 min, no special needs (can go in either room)\n- Event B: 30 min, no special needs (can go in either room)\n- Event C: 60 min, preferredRoom Room 1\n\nIf we sort A and B before C (same level), A goes to Room 1 at 0-30, B goes to Room 2 at 0-30. Then C tries Room 1 (preferred), but earliest is at 45 (after A + gap), and 45+60=105 which may exceed endTime. So C goes to unscheduled.\n\nBut optimal would be: A in Room 1, C in Room 1 at 0-60 (preferred!), B in Room 2 at 0-30.\n\nSo the constrainedness ordering helps: C has a preferred room but A and B don't. Hmm, but preferredRoom isn't really about constrainedness in terms of room count.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The preferred room is a hint, not a hard constraint. So if C has preferredRoom Room 1, it *prefers* Room 1 but can go to Room 2. So in the scenario above, C can go to either room.\n\nBut what if C really can only go in Room 1 (because it has 10 attendees and Room 2 has capacity 5)? Then C is more constrained (1 room option vs 2 for A and B), so it would be placed first. Good.\n\nLet me add preferredRoom as a tiebreaker in the constrainedness: events with a preferred room get a small priority boost (try preferred room first in the slot search).\n\nI think my current algorithm already